<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James I of Aragon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1208–<a href="/years/1276">1276</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as James the Conqueror</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James I, called the Conqueror, was king of Aragon and count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> from 1213 to 1276. He conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a> in 1229 and the Muslim kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> in 1238, creating new realms with their own laws within the Crown of Aragon. In the Treaty of Corbeil of 1258 he gave up most claims north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>. His chronicle, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llibre-dels-fets">Book of Deeds</a>, is the first royal autobiography in Catalan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-i-of-aragon.jpg" alt="James I of Aragon" width="162" height="353" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vidal de Canellas y cancillería real del rey de Aragón.; derivative work by Escarlati · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jaime_I_de_Arag%C3%B3n.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
